WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:04.620 --> 00:00:11.400
School choice has proven consistently popular among one very dependable constituency: parents.

00:00:11.400 --> 00:00:15.120
And unpopular among another: unionized teachers.

00:00:15.120 --> 00:00:18.320
The rapid spread of school choice over the past 20 years.

00:00:18.320 --> 00:00:23.580
Has triggered an increasingly fierce union pushback setting up an inevitable clash.

00:00:23.580 --> 00:00:28.120
Enter an insidious yet helpfully descriptive new term in policymaking:

00:00:28.120 --> 00:00:29.400
Controlled Choice.

00:00:29.400 --> 00:00:33.080
Yes, you parents still get to choose your desired schools

00:00:33.080 --> 00:00:36.940
but public education bureaucrats get to control the final outcome

00:00:36.940 --> 00:00:41.300
and lately from New York to San Francisco, Charlotte to Polk County

00:00:41.300 --> 00:00:45.900
the only criteria that matters is not student performance but race.

00:00:45.900 --> 00:00:49.120
'New York City schools got a little less segregated this week.'

00:00:49.120 --> 00:00:53.000
my local city councilman in Brooklyn wrote at the beginning of this school year.

00:00:53.000 --> 00:00:54.840
'The winner is everyone.'

00:00:54.840 --> 00:00:56.620
Well, about that...

00:00:56.620 --> 00:01:02.960
In 2007 the Supreme Court barred school districts from assigning students to schools based on race.

00:01:02.960 --> 00:01:06.100
so instead they use income as the primary determinant

00:01:06.100 --> 00:01:10.280
and characterized the resulting admissions changes as desegregation.

00:01:10.280 --> 00:01:14.580
The goal is to have poorer kids homeless kids and english-language learners

00:01:14.580 --> 00:01:16.580
spread out evenly among schools.

00:01:16.580 --> 00:01:20.720
While crossing their fingers that parents won't bolt and that all schools will improve.

00:01:20.720 --> 00:01:26.080
When this system was tried in my home district in Brooklyn It ended up being hellishly complicated.

00:01:26.080 --> 00:01:31.240
The team that designed New York City's school picking algorithm literally won the Nobel Prize for economics

00:01:31.240 --> 00:01:35.700
though, fun fact, one of those designers didn't like where the system placed his own kid

00:01:35.700 --> 00:01:39.420
so he opted out and chose a charter and I don't blame him one bit.

00:01:39.420 --> 00:01:40.740
The good news this year

00:01:40.740 --> 00:01:45.240
is that kids from communities that had never even considered some of the high-performing schools

00:01:45.240 --> 00:01:48.200
both applied to and were admitted in higher numbers.

00:01:48.200 --> 00:01:50.040
School choice for the win!

00:01:50.040 --> 00:01:53.860
But turns out parents don't much enjoy being controlled.

00:01:53.860 --> 00:01:57.180
One size fits all schemes may look good on paper to progressives

00:01:57.180 --> 00:01:59.480
but when applied to their own special snowflakes

00:01:59.480 --> 00:02:03.880
suddenly lefty parents start sounding as skeptical of central planning as FA Hayek

00:02:03.880 --> 00:02:06.080
or at least quietly eyeing the exits.

00:02:06.080 --> 00:02:09.760
After more than a half decade of sustained increasing enrollment

00:02:09.760 --> 00:02:14.680
my district sixth grade class shrunk by a whopping seven percent this year.

00:02:14.680 --> 00:02:17.880
What happens in Brooklyn alas doesn't stay in Brooklyn.

00:02:17.880 --> 00:02:22.500
The same policy process is underway in Queens and parents there are already revolting.

00:02:22.500 --> 00:02:26.680
The New York City School Chancellor is not winning many new converts to these changes

00:02:26.680 --> 00:02:29.500
with this habit of calling skeptical parents racist.

00:02:39.440 --> 00:02:44.540
Meanwhile the city is considering a mandate that would require every traditional public school

00:02:44.540 --> 00:02:49.760
have roughly the same racial ethnic and socio-economic makeup within ten years.

00:02:49.760 --> 00:02:51.160
Good luck with that.

00:02:51.160 --> 00:02:56.660
Parents of means can always take the expensive options of private school or physically moving to a new district.

00:02:56.660 --> 00:03:01.680
But the main off ramp for the non-rich has been New York's extensive charter school system

00:03:01.680 --> 00:03:03.860
now, just guess what kind of schools

00:03:03.860 --> 00:03:07.540
New York politicians are placing an artificial cap on?

00:03:07.540 --> 00:03:11.040
So how has controlled choice worked out there in the world?

00:03:11.040 --> 00:03:14.900
The Cato Institute's David Armor looked at a half dozen controlled choice districts

00:03:14.900 --> 00:03:17.520
and found that enrollment went down,

00:03:17.520 --> 00:03:21.380
richer families fled and schools didn't even end up more integrated.

00:03:21.380 --> 00:03:24.300
So a friendly reminder here from the People's Republic of Brooklyn:

00:03:24.300 --> 00:03:27.400
controlled choice is an oxymoron.

00:03:27.400 --> 00:03:30.560
top-down systems look bad from the bottom up,

00:03:30.560 --> 00:03:34.960
choking off supply then browbeating demand is no way to run a service,

00:03:34.960 --> 00:03:39.840
New York and other cities would be better off allowing more competitors to provide public education

00:03:39.840 --> 00:03:42.560
and more choice to meet the idiosyncratic needs

00:03:42.560 --> 00:03:45.860
of a challenging diverse and high achieving city.

